  "conclusions": [
    "The cheap non-neural gate and neural gate form a Pareto trade-off rather than one universally dominating the other.",
    "On the new Silesia holdout the neural gate improves mean payload by about 0.072 bpb, but is about 4.2x slower in both encode and decode and requires a 603105-byte shared checkpoint.",
    "At the observed Silesia mean bpb advantage, about 67.1 MB (64.0 MiB) must be coded under the same shared checkpoint merely to amortize checkpoint bytes.",
    "The neural gate remains highly valuable on the self-v2 domain (1.7095 bpb vs 3.7002 cheap), while the cheap gate can win or tie on some external binary data and requires no learned model.",
    "The universal POLLICINO path should therefore keep the cheap adaptive/classical gate available without neural dependencies; the neural gate should be an optional shared specialist selected when model availability and expected amortization justify it."
  ],
